+# FAQ for newcomers
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+Install it with pipx (Python 3.11 or newer): `pipx install tally-toolkit`. Check it with `tallyctl version`. If the
+command is not found, run `pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release. The old name works as an alias for one more release and is
+then removed, so use the new name.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+Port 8731. The old port 8080 is closed since the 2026-09 release, so anything still using it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+Run `tallyctl cache clear` to clear all regions, or `tallyctl cache clear --region NAME` for one. The old purge command
+was removed.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes without acknowledgement. The page goes from the primary to the secondary on-call, then to the engineering
+manager.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1, meaning customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2 incidents
+do not page; the primary picks them up in the morning.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Run the drain restart first: `tallyctl restart ingest --drain`. It parks the stuck batch in the dead-letter table and
+starts the consumer clean. A plain restart picks the same batch up again. Look for the cause afterwards.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Confirm the expiry with `tallyctl certs list`, renew with `tallyctl certs renew api`, then restart with
+`tallyctl restart api`. The new certificate is only served after the restart.
